WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:07.200
Much of the remainder of this section of the
course will cover lists and list management.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.820
A list is a set of multiple values assigned
to a single variable.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.630
Lists have square brackets surrounding the
values within the list.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.690
Later in this section, we will cover different
types of lists and their uses, but in this

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:25.930
topic, we will cover indexing, which is the
positioning of each element within a list.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.730
Take this 122-indexing file, for example.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:35.310
Here, line 1 lists categories related to Ancient
Near East history eras.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:39.660
If this code is to be part of a quiz-based
game, people using the app will need to choose

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.630
a category at any given time.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.649
Line 2 has a print statement to print the
categories variable.

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:48.690
Let’s run this code and see the result.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:55.150
The result is that we see all the categories.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.130
But what if we just want to see one category?

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:04.580
I will copy the code on line 2 and paste it
on line 4.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:13.330
Then, I will add a 1 inside square brackets
inside the closing parenthesis to indicate

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:19.110
I want to print just the first category, the
Early Bronze Age.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:27.630
I will run the code, and we see that what
we wanted did not print.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.350
The Middle Bronze Age printed instead.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.460
Why is that?

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:37.590
To understand why, we need to understand a
basic programming concept about indexes and

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.909
similar list-based situations.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:48.270
Most programming languages, including Python,
are zero-based with lists and their values.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:56.170
By zero-based, we mean that the first value
in the list is at index zero, the next one

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:59.590
is index one, and so forth.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:06.200
Thus, to return the first item in the list,
this number for the index needs to be zero.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:16.670
I will make that change, rerun the code, and
now we will see our desired Early Bronze Age

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.140
value.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:23.080
To further demonstrate the zero-based point,
I will change the number for the category

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:36.390
to 2, rerun the code, and Late Bronze Age,
the third item in the list, is returned.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:41.470
One aspect of indexing that causes consternation
for some is trying to figure out how to return

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.170
the last or maybe second-to-last item in the
list.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.860
If this list had 20 items or so, one would
need to count the number of items in the list

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:56.890
to return the correct item at or near the
end of the list, which is a cumbersome process

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:02.860
and can lead to inaccuracies in obtaining
the correct indexed value from a list.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:08.670
However, Python can easily obtain the last
item in the list by returning an index of

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:15.640
negative one, as I will do here on line 4,
and then rerunning the code.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:21.530
And we see the last element in the list.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:27.110
What do you think will happen if I change
this index to -2?

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:34.450
Upon running this code, we see the second-to-last
item in the list.

